      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Planning for Local LLMs: Separating Allocations",
      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical checklist for VRAM planning when running LLMs locally. It's crucial to understand that no specific models or hardware were tested; this is purely a conceptual guide based on common allocation needs.\n\nThe core idea is to break down VRAM usage into distinct categories: model weights, KV cache, and other runtime overhead. For a hypothetical 12 GB GPU, this separation is vital for preventing Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors.\n\nModel weights are typically the largest consumer. The KV cache size scales with context length and batch size, becoming significant for longer sequences. Other allocations include activations, optimizer states (during training/finetuning, though less relevant for inference-focused planning), and framework overhead. \n\nUnderstanding these components allows for more informed decisions about which models or configurations might fit within available VRAM. For instance, a smaller model with a large KV cache might consume more VRAM than a larger model with a small cache. This framework is intended to facilitate discussion on pre-computation VRAM budgeting.",
