    {
      "title": "Conceptual VRAM Breakdown for Local LLM Inference (12GB GPU Scenario)",
      "body": "Considering a hypothetical 12 GB GPU, this guide proposes a structured approach to VRAM planning for local LLM inference. It is important to note that this is a theoretical exercise, and no actual models or hardware were evaluated.\n\nThe primary goal is to differentiate between key VRAM consumers: model weights, the Key-Value (KV) cache, and miscellaneous runtime allocations. This separation is essential for estimating feasibility and avoiding OOM errors.\n\nModel weights represent the static parameters of the LLM. The KV cache, however, is dynamic and grows with the sequence length and batch size, significantly impacting VRAM usage during inference. Other runtime allocations can include intermediate activations and framework overhead. \n\nBy conceptually separating these elements, users can better anticipate resource requirements. For example, a model requiring substantial KV cache due to long context windows might be unsuitable, even if its base weights are relatively small. This checklist aims to provide a framework for discussion on VRAM management strategies.",
